Rust edition of the Loongson-3 CPUFreq driver -- read this before using it
=========================================================================

This package ships the loongson3_cpufreq driver rewritten in Rust for Linux.
The sources and the build helper scripts are installed into

    /usr/src/loongson3-cpufreq-7.2.8/

A Rust kernel module cannot be built the way a C one can
--------------------------------------------------------

`dkms build` (or `make -C /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build M=... modules`) works
by compiling the module against a *linux-headers* package.  That is enough for a
C module, but not for a Rust one, for two independent reasons:

 1. A Rust module is compiled against the kernel's compiled Rust abstractions
    (`rust/libkernel.rmeta`, `rust/libcore.rmeta`, `rust/bindings/...`).  The
    headers package does not contain them; distributions only build the C parts
    for out-of-tree modules.

 2. Every Rust symbol is mangled with a hash of the compiler and the exact
    compilation flags.  A module built with a different rustc -- even a
    different build of the same upstream release -- refers to symbols that the
    running kernel does not export, and `insmod` fails with "Unknown symbol".

Because of that this package deliberately does not build the module in its
postinst: `AUTOINSTALL` is left unset in dkms.conf, so DKMS only registers the
module.  Building it automatically would fail on a stock system and would be
retried on every kernel upgrade.

How to build it
---------------

    apt install linux-headers-$(uname -r)     # for Module.symvers
    cd /usr/src/loongson3-cpufreq-7.2.8
    tools/setup-rfl.sh                        # no root needed, one-off
    . tools/rfl-env.sh
    make
    sudo make install

`tools/setup-rfl.sh` downloads -- it never installs -- the kernel source package
and the exact rustc/bindgen toolchain the running kernel was built with, into
~/.cache/loongson3-cpufreq-rfl, compiles the kernel's Rust support there and
copies the running kernel's Module.symvers into the tree.  It prints the
commands to run afterwards.  Re-running it is cheap.

To drive DKMS with that tree instead of the headers package:

    . tools/rfl-env.sh
    dkms build   loongson3-cpufreq/7.2.8 --kernelsourcedir="$KDIR"
    dkms install loongson3-cpufreq/7.2.8 --kernelsourcedir="$KDIR"

Note that the tree has to be prepared again for every new kernel release, since
the Rust symbol hashes and the ABI change with it.

